Section 3: Advanced Joins
In this section, I learned about the join operator, which offers:
Choice of what happens when records don't match
Ability to keep records from one or both tables without matches
Multiple join types for different analytical needs
The kind= parameter specifies the join type.

And given a sample query:
Table1
| join kind=inner (
    Table2
) on $left.field1 == $right.field2

And told that an inner join only keeps records that match in BOTH tables.
So this query would take only the matching columns from Table 1 and Table 2.

Then, we are taught that the first kind of join we can do is a leftouter join. This join takes the left table (main table) and adds data from the right table if there are matching keys. It doesn't drop any data, and you are guaranteed to have at least the same number of rows you started with.
Email
| where sender == "diego_hernandez@whiskersandwonders.org"
| join kind=leftouter Employees on $left.recipient == $right.email_addr
| project-reorder timestamp, sender, recipient, ip_addr

So this query will go to the Email table and find results that Diego sent. Then, it would use a leftouter join to take the matching data from Email and Employees, which would be the recipient email addresses (looking at internal emails sent). Then, the first columns will be timestamp, sender (Diego), recipient (internal), and the recipient's IP. After that is the rest of the info from both tables.
How many results do we get back after the join?
Answer: 15

Unlike leftouter, an inner join only keeps records where there's a match in both tables. If a recipient doesn't exist in the Employees table, that row gets dropped entirely.
Email
| where sender == "diego_hernandez@whiskersandwonders.org"
| join kind=inner Employees on $left.recipient == $right.email_addr
| project-reorder timestamp, sender, recipient, ip_addr

This will do the same thing as before, but instead of returning all info from both tables, it will only fill in cells with data that matches from both tables. The other columns will still exist, they will just return empty.
How many results do we get back now?
Answer: 7

A rightouter join flips the logic of leftouter. Instead of keeping all rows from the left table, it keeps all rows from the right table and only adds matching data from the left.
Email
| where sender == "diego_hernandez@whiskersandwonders.org"
| join kind=rightouter Employees on $left.recipient == $right.email_addr
| project-reorder timestamp, sender, recipient, ip_addr, email_addr, name

For employees who received an email from Diego, you'll see the email data populated. For everyone else, the email columns (timestamp, sender, recipient, etc.) will be empty or null.
This seems counterproductive for our current purposes, so they gave us a use case: "Which employees did Diego not email?" - A rightouter join gives you every employee, and you can then filter for the ones with no matching email:
Email
| where sender == "diego_hernandez@whiskersandwonders.org"
| join kind=rightouter Employees on $left.recipient == $right.email_addr
| where isempty(sender)
| project name, email_addr
How many results do we get back this time?
Answer: 30

The next query shown is:
Email
| where links has "update-cdn-service.xyz"
| mv-expand links to typeof(string)
| join kind=leftouter Employees on $left.recipient == $right.email_addr
| join kind=leftouter (    
    ProxyEvents
    | where url has "update-cdn-service.xyz"
) on $left.ip_addr == $right.src_ip, $left.links == $right.url
| project timestamp, sender, recipient, links, clicked_at = timestamp1

To paraphrase their explanation, this query will find emails with the suspicious link. Then, it will change that link to a string and leftouter join it with the recipient's email from Employees. Then, using another leftouter join, finds the URL from ProxyEvents and matches up the IP in Employees to the source IP in ProxyEvents and the links in Email to the URL in ProxyEvents. Then, the first columns are timestamp, sender, recipient, links, and timestamp from ProxyEvents for when the link was clicked.
Then, it gives another line to add to find who DIDN'T click:
| where isempty(clicked_at)

What percent of users clicked on the link?
4 did not and there were 4 total, so 0%.

Use a leftouter join and isempty() to find employees who received the phishing email with domain api-sync-updates.top but did NOT click.
